  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\nA cloudy start to Monday with remnant moisture from Sunday's \nstorms will stunt warming into the early afternoon hours. \nMoisture will pool ahead of a southward sagging cold front, \nreally a dew point front, as a weak 700 mb wave slides along the\nboundary this afternoon. This lift will be enough to spark off \nscattered showers and a few thunderstorms within the plenty \nmoisture-rich airmass still in place on the warm side of the \nboundary with dew points in the low 70s and PWAT value of 1.86 \ninches on our 12z sounding.\n\nModification of the profile to warm surface temperatures a few \ndegrees does suggest MLCAPE values nearing 1000 J/kg with effective \nshear values in the 15-20 knot range, though with deep layer \nmoisture, the primary hazard today will be locally heavy downpours. \nCloud bearing wind vectors sit roughly parallel to the southward \nmoving boundary, so will have to watch for an areas that could sit \nunder a heavier shower or storm, but motion should be enough to \npreclude a notable flood concern. HREF probability of >1\" of rain in \nan hour peaks at 40-60% south of Pittsburgh with a ~30% chance of \n>2\" total through tonight. Will especially keep an eye on areas hit \nhardest in the past 24 hours (Wetzel and Marion Counties) where one \nhour flash flood guidance ranges from 0.90 to 1.25 inches. Elsewhere \nto the north of Pittsburgh, most of the day should be dry as \ncool/dry advection in northerly flow is providing more of a fall \nlook to the satellite imagery with cooler 850 temps less than 10C \nmoving over a warm Lake Erie providing some low lake-induced clouds. \nThose should also erode into this evening with increased mixing and \nadvection of much drier deep column air.\n\nTuesday will be dry areawide with quasi-zonal mid-level flow and \nweak surface high pressure providing a comfortable mid-August day \nwith highs in the upper 70s and low 60s dew points.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\nOur one day of relief from showers and storms quickly fades \ninto the past as yet another cold front moves through on \nWednesday. Return flow will pull better Gulf moisture back into \nthe local area again, though not to the extent we saw over the \npast weekend, with ensemble mean PWAT values climbing back up to\n~1.5\" and dew points to the upper 60s. Right entrance region \njet dynamics and weak PVA will aid in synoptic ascent, and a 40+\nknot mid-level jet increase deep layer shear. That said, \nsurface CAPE appears to be a primary limiting factor with NBM \nmean around 300-600 J/kg and only getting to 1000 J/kg with the \n90th percentile values contingent low probability clearing in \nthe morning and push of higher dew points. CIPS analogs is not \nexcited about any severe or flooding with the front, so we \nshouldn't be looking at any significant threat either way on \nWednesday.\n\nThe question beyond that point is the progression, or lackthereof, \nof the front. Mid-level flow turns more zonal and subsequently more \nparallel to the front suggesting it slows/stalls absent any \nmechanism to give it a kick to keep moving. NBM dew point spread as \na proxy for boundary placement basically suggests it could stall \nanywhere from I-80 to I-70, though ensemble clusters lean toward \nslow progression south Thursday and Friday focusing the best rain \nchances along and south Pittsburgh by the second half of the day on \nThursday. Obviously will have to watch for repeated rounds of \nrain over the same areas, but generally weak forcing might lend \nmore toward scattered, diurnally driven showers and storms with \nthe highest coverage on Thursday afternoon as a shortwave tracks\nacross the boundary. NBM 90th percentile 24 hour totals sit \nabout as high as 1.75\", so the rainfall totals ceiling should \nbe a bit lower than we've seen in the past week. Machine \nlearning pegs a lower end flood risk.\n\nSaid shortwave likely gives the stalled boundary the punch it \nneeds to exit our area by the weekend, though a troughy pattern\nlikely keeps us active with longer range AI & ML guidance \nsuggesting our next severe weather risk popping up on Saturday.",
